# Normalise line endings in the repository, check out native on each machine.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout stages CRLF and every file in the tree shows as modified the
# first time anyone opens it on Linux — which is also what CI runs on. `text=auto` stores LF and
# hands out whatever the platform expects.
* text=auto

# Files whose endings are not negotiable.
*.go        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.proto     text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
# Golden files are compared byte-for-byte against renderer output, which
# emits LF. text=auto's CRLF checkout on Windows fails every golden test
# locally while CI (Linux, LF) passes — found 2026-08-16.
*.golden    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run on the deployment box, and a CR at the end of the shebang line is a
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" that names the wrong problem. Edited on Windows,
# executed on Ubuntu — the ending is not a per-machine preference.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf

# Git hooks have no extension, so the *.sh rule above does not reach them. They
# are executed by sh on every commit, on Windows and on CI, and a CR at the end
# of the shebang produces "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" — an error that names the
# wrong problem and stops every commit until someone works out why.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The Makefile is not negotiable in a stronger sense: a recipe line arriving with CRLF makes GNU
# make pass a trailing \r to the shell, which fails as `command not found: go\r` — an error naming
# a command nobody typed, sending people to look for a broken toolchain.
Makefile    text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles are read by a Linux daemon; a CRLF in a RUN line ends up inside the command.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception: Windows-only here, and some hosts still mis-parse bare LFs.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Generated protobuf code. Marked so a diff collapses by default and does not drown a review —
# it is regenerated by buf, never hand-edited.
gen/**/*.go linguist-generated=true -diff

# Feed fixtures are recordings, not text. `text=auto` would rewrite CRLFs inside a file whose whole
# purpose is to be byte-identical to what a real server sent, and a non-UTF-8 fixture would have to
# be guessed at — a wrong guess silently destroys the evidence. Marking them binary keeps a fixture
# a fixture.
testdata/**/*.raw   binary

# Golden feed files are compared byte for byte. Any rewriting at all defeats them.
testdata/golden/**  -text

# Binary, so git never tries to be clever with it.
*.png       binary
*.webp      binary
*.wasm      binary
*.gz        binary
*.db        binary
